    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **280 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented a per-city owner cap that rejects owner creation with **400 Bad Request** once a city already contains 8 owners.\n\nFollowing the existing OfficeFloor function-injection patterns (mirroring the daily-limit check already in the pipeline):\n\n**New files:**\n- `EnforceCityOwnerLimit.java` \u2014 the function step. Counts existing owners whose city matches the candidate's (case- and whitespace-insensitive, reusing the same `matchKey` normalization as `NormalizeCity`); throws `CityOwnerLimitExceededException` when the count is \u2265 8 (`MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY`). Blank/null cities are skipped.\n- `CityOwnerLimitExceededException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`).\n- `CityOwnerLimitExceededExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 escalation handler responding 400 with a `ProblemDetail`.\n- `officefloor/escalation/...CityOwnerLimitExceededException.yml` \u2014 wires the handler.\n\n**Modified:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `enforceCityLimit` step (under `transaction` governance) between `enforceDailyLimit` and `assignMembership`, i.e. before the owner is saved.\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.java` \u2014 added two tests: one seeding 8 owners in a city and asserting the 9th POST returns 400, and one seeding 7 and asserting the 8th succeeds (201). Both use unique city names so seed data doesn't interfere, with distinct names/telephones so the existing uniqueness check isn't tripped.",
